Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Skyworks**, an open launch-pad hangar whose turbine
spins fast overhead while its launch door raises and lowers, as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}}
opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting
discrete opaque cells** and you **author its turbine and launch-door animations** as
F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Skyworks is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the pad into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a launch pad with a
spinning turbine and a raising door needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json`
— together with the rig you define are your submission.

`voxel-anim` places **discrete opaque cubes**: you paint cells with `set-voxel`,
`fill-box`, box strokes, 3D lines, and spheres, clear them, and mirror across a
plane. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so create a part before you sculpt
into it; each part is sculpted **separately** in its own preview and log. The volume
starts **empty**. Each call **records only** — it appends the operation to that part's
log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part
previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Each part is sculpted in
the shared volume's coordinates, positioned where it sits on the assembled pad.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name pad
voxel-anim fill-box --part pad --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 4 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring each required
animation's F-curves with `define-animation` / `add-keyframe`), and
`voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is
the authoritative contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to
see that part's progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
pad composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views
are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the turbine sits centered
high on its mast and the door seats in the front face. Because each part is sculpted
in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the
finished pad; the scene simply composes them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the Skyworks up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a broad open masonry pad with a center mast, a
bladed turbine spinning high on that mast, and a launch door set in the front face.
**Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation
subcommands: `turbine_spin` (an even, continuous turbine sweep about its vertical
axis) and `launch_door_raise` (the door sliding up, holding open, then lowering back —
eased so it carries weight, not a linear slide). Both play on their own
(`auto_play`), so the Skyworks runs without any caller. The animations are the fixed
contract — produce both, by those names; the parts and joints that realize them are
yours. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part `.glb`
geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is scored as empty (`voxel-anim
render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation
<name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an animation to check the motion).
When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are
your finished submission.
